reims-vgpu: the render Store copies and converts in claimed row bands.

A native-float render Store of the presented RGBA16F front surface (25 MB)
copied its readback into the guest's pages on the drain thread with ordinary
stores while a second thread, itself starting four, converted the whole
readback for the present's stash. A recorded live second on the development
host (1.2.1 engine, swiping) shows 4.5-4.8 ms a Store for that publish with
the conversion beside it (native_float_publish_us), and 2.3-2.9 ms for the
conversion (store_capture_convert_us): the two passes read the staging
buffer twice and the guest pages once more for ownership, and contend for
memory.

Now PreparedNativeImageWrite::write_from_banded cuts the rows into parts
(eight per thread) that up to four threads, the drain's own included, claim
in turn, so a slower core takes fewer. Each part copies its rows with
streaming stores (no read of the guest's lines first), and after each block
of 16 rows hands those rows to its sink while they are in the core's cache:
the Stasher converts them into its part of the stash with
convert_band_streamed. The stash buffer is taken before the readback and the
frame is stashed after the staging lease has ended (Stasher::commit, which
stashes nothing when any part refused or did not run).

The guest's bytes are those the one-thread copy leaves, every row at its
offset and the padding untouched; parts run one after another on the drain
when the pieces are not provably disjoint (two guest pages naming one host
page) or no thread starts. REIMS_VGPU_STORE_PUBLISH_BANDS=off restores the
copy with the conversion beside it. Census: native_image_band_parts_n,
native_image_band_threads_n, store_capture_banded_n;
store_capture_convert_us is now the parts' summed conversion time.

Offline (store_banded_timing: the vphone frame into 16 KiB pages scattered
through guest RAM, with its stash, caches evicted before each run, medians of
31 alternating, three runs on a shared host): beside 3.5-3.9 ms, banded on
four threads 2.5-2.8 ms, on one 3.7-4.1 ms; the banded copy alone 2.0-2.2 ms
on four threads. Not measured on a live phone.

